Phase 4, S Pen (docs/phase4-spen-scoping.md). Mainline already has the
right driver family for this device's Wacom EMR digitizer -
drivers/input/touchscreen/wacom_w9000.c, confirmed field-for-field
against this device's own downstream driver (same query command, same
byte offsets for max_x/max_y/pressure/fw_version). Adds:

  - a new "wacom,w9021-gts7l" variant with the longer query/coordinate
    message lengths this chip's response format needs (it also carries
    module/bootloader version, tilt, and height fields the shorter
    w9002/w9007a variants don't have) - the two compile-time buffer
    maximums are bumped to fit;
  - tilt-axis (ABS_TILT_X/ABS_TILT_Y) reporting, which the driver
    didn't have at all before this - added following the exact same
    conditional pattern the driver already uses for ABS_DISTANCE.
    Offsets (query response 0x0b/0x0c for the max range, coordinate-
    message bytes 8/9 for the live value, both signed) are pulled
    directly from the downstream driver's register map
    (references/gts7l/drivers/input/wacom/wacom_reg.h's
    EPEN_REG_TILT_X/TILT_Y and the runtime coordinate-report parsing in
    wacom_i2c.c), not guessed;
  - a per-variant power_on_delay_ms/query_retry_delay_ms (0 = driver's
    original defaults, existing variants unaffected) and
    force_power_cycle (false by default, existing variants unaffected)
    - all tested on real hardware and individually ruled out as fixing
    this chip's remaining non-response (see status below), but kept as
    real, harmless robustness improvements matching downstream's own
    documented behavior.

The exact public Wacom part number for this chip is unconfirmed (no
public source found for it - see the scoping doc) - "gts7l" is used in
the compatible string rather than asserting an unverified model name.

Status (2026-09-21): built and tested on real hardware multiple times.
The digitizer enumerates correctly on the I2C bus (`17-0056`) and the
driver binds, but consistently fails its query command with -ENXIO (no
I2C ack at all, not just bad data). Wiring this chip's real AVDD rail
(PM8150 LDO13, see kernel/dts/sm8250-samsung-gts7l.dts and
docs/phase4-spen-scoping.md) caused a real hardware brownout the first
few attempts - root-caused and fixed (a mainline regulator-core
interaction, not a problem with this driver - see the DTS commit for
the full trail). With that fixed, no more brownout, but the chip still
doesn't respond.

Three independently well-reasoned hypotheses have since been tested
on real hardware and cleanly ruled out, each with an identical -ENXIO
result:
  1. Power-on timing (500ms settle + spaced retries vs the driver's
     stock 200ms/back-to-back).
  2. flash-mode-gpios polarity (tested both GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH and
     GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW).
  3. Power-cycle sequencing (force_power_cycle above) - matched
     exactly against Samsung's own downstream driver for this chip
     (references/gts7l/drivers/input/wacom/wacom_i2c.c,
     wacom_power()/wacom_reset_hw(): explicitly forces a real
     disable->100ms->enable->200ms cycle, never trusting a bare
     enable() to have toggled real hardware when the rail can already
     read back as enabled via RPMh aggregate voting) - confirmed via
     direct source reading, not guessed, and it still didn't work.

What's next needs either the chip's real datasheet (not publicly
available for Wacom EMR digitizers) or a live UART capture of what the
chip itself reports at power-up - this project's own prior research
(docs/uart-debug-research.md) documents a real method for the latter
but it was never built/tested; it needs physical hardware (a bare
USB-C breakout board, a ~619kOhm resistor, a USB-to-TTL serial adapter)
not available this session.

